{
  "corpus": "scripts/demo-corpus",
  "about": [
    "Queries a colleague would actually type, each with the records that answer it. Targets are named",
    "by `<corpus file>#<record key>` and resolved to stored ids at run time, so the set survives a",
    "reload (ids are ULIDs minted per load).",
    "",
    "Written deliberately in words the target record does NOT use, wherever a person would plausibly",
    "ask it that way — a question copied from its answer measures string matching, not retrieval. Some",
    "queries do share vocabulary with their target, because a real question sometimes does; a set that",
    "was uniformly paraphrased would flatter the vector half exactly as a lexical set flatters BM25.",
    "",
    "Relevance is binary and generous: a record counts if a person asking this would be glad to see it.",
    "Where a corpus contains two records that disagree, BOTH are relevant — surfacing one and hiding",
    "the other is the failure yoke exists to prevent.",
    "",
    "Two query SHAPES, and the split matters: `question` is a sentence, which is how an agent's user asks,",
    "and `keywords` is one to three terms, which is how a person searches a wiki. Reported separately,",
    "because a set of only one shape makes one retriever look broken when what differs is the query."
  ],
